index 631a236..9941c00 100644
--- a/deploy/README.md
+++ b/deploy/README.md
@@ -201,6 +201,32 @@ which could also change the account's email or delete it. The account always com
 token. Guests have nothing to manage: their progress stays in the browser, and a guest result is
 stored only if they typed a name, with no link to anything else.
 
+## Another site on the same login (levelup)
+
+The levelup site (https://levelup.skillrealm.dev) signs in with this user pool, through its own app
+client (`LevelupUserPoolClient`, output `LevelupClientId`), and keeps its progress here:
+`GET /apps/{app}/state` and `PUT /apps/{app}/state`, authorized by the same Cognito JWT.
+
+- **Isolation.** The row lives in `ProgressTable` under `userId = "<sub>#app:<app>"` with only
+  `state` and `updatedAt`. It never has `scores`, so the leaderboard never lists it, and the
+  get-certified `/state` blob is never read or written by these routes (and the reverse).
+- **Allowlist.** `APPS` in `lambda/domain/apps.mjs` (today `levelup`). Any other name is a 404
+  `unknown_app`. A body must be a JSON object of at most 300 KB (413 `too_large`).
+- **Account data.** `GET /me/export` adds `apps: { levelup: <blob or null> }` and `DELETE /me`
+  removes every app row before the Cognito user.
+- **Parameter.** `LevelupOrigin` (default the URL above; empty turns the client, its CORS origins
+  and its audience entry off). `deploy.sh` passes it on every run (`LEVELUP_ORIGIN=` to disable) and
+  adds it and `http://localhost:3000` to the CORS origins it applies after the stack update.
+- **Cost.** A second Cognito app client and two routes on the existing API and Lambda: $0 extra
+  fixed. Per request about $1.00 per million API calls plus Lambda and a DynamoDB on-demand read or
+  write (about $0.125 per million read units, $0.625 per million write units, one unit per 1 KB
+  written; approximate us-east-1 list prices, assumptions to re-check, not verified when written).
+  Typical blobs are a few KB, so about $1 per million saves.
+  Worst case: the throttle is per route, not per account: 2 requests per second sustained (burst 10)
+  is at most about 5.2M writes a month. If every one were a 300 KB blob that is about $1,000 a month
+  in DynamoDB writes, but it needs a signed-in caller sustaining the limit all month; the
+  `MonthlyCostBudget` alert fires first. Lower the throttle or the 300 KB cap if that is too much.
+
 ## Testimonials
 
 Learners' stories at `#/testimonials`, on the home page and on each cert's landing page.
